-- title:
--   Level-ℓ formal family composed with p_ℓ equals tower family
-- statement:
--   The setting is the $r$-adic (Čerednik–Drinfeld) comparison for a tower of moduli of fake elliptic curves, and the assertion is a compatibility of two families of nilpotent-thickening points.
--
--   Arithmetic data. Two distinct primes $r$ and $\bar r$ (`hrr`), and a non-zero natural number $N$ which is squarefree (`hN`) and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrN`, `hrbarN`).
--
--   Local base. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), $\pi$-adic completeness (`hcomplete`), residue cardinality $\#(\mathcal O/\pi)=r$ (`hres`) and $(r)=(\pi)$ (`hunr`), together with a fraction field $K_0$ of $\mathcal O$ of characteristic zero. A second $\mathcal O$-algebra $Onr$, a characteristic-zero domain, with an $\mathcal O$-automorphism $Fr$, complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal being maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $Onr$ satisfying a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $Onr$ having a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x)\equiv x^{r}$ modulo $\pi$ (`hFr`); thus $Onr/\pi$ plays the role of the algebraic closure of $\mathbb F_r$ and $Fr$ of its Frobenius. A homomorphism $vdet:\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)\to\mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) with `hvdet`: $vdet(g)=n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ for some $u\in\mathcal O^\times$.
--
--   Indefinite side and coarse moduli. Rationals $a,b$ with `hB`: $0<a$ or $0<b$, and for every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the completion of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ at $v$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ (`hΛ`). A scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with a point map $pt$ and `h𝒳 : IsCoarseModuli`, i.e. $\mathcal X$ coarsely represents fake elliptic curves with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure (isomorphism invariance, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points, and the universal property).
--
--   Fine moduli with full level $n$. A natural number $n\ge 3$ (`hn`) coprime to $N$ (`hnN`) and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$ (`hrn`, `hrbarn`); a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point map $ptF$ and `hM : IsFineModuli` for pairs (curve, full level-$n$ structure); a group $G$ acting by automorphisms $\rho$ of $M$ with labels $\chi:G\to\Lambda$ and `hρ : IsLevelTwistAction` (the action is over the base, realises twisting of level structures on points, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, injective and surjective modulo $n$). A forgetful morphism $p:M\to\mathcal X$ over the base (`hp`), invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`) and compatible with the two point maps (`hp_pt`).
--
--   Hecke tower and Atkin–Lehner data. For every prime $\ell$ different from $r$ and $\bar r$, a coarse moduli scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ with point map $ptT$ and `h𝒴 : IsCoarseModuliT` for curves with an extra level-$\ell$ subgroup, together with the two degeneracy morphisms $d_0,d_1$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) described on points by `hd₀` (forget the subgroup) and `hd₁` (divide by the subgroup). Morphisms $ar,arbar:\mathcal X\to\mathcal X$ over the base (`harf`, `harbarf`) realising on points the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   Definite side. Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and the completion at a finite place is a division algebra exactly at the places above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$ (`hΛ₁`, `hR₁`, `hRΛ₁`); an adelic unit $n_1$ in `primeHeckeSet R₁ r` (`hn₁`) such that `meetOrder R₁ n₁` is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$ (`hι₀`); a place $v$ above $r$ (`hv`). A subgroup $\Gamma t$ which by `hΓt` coincides with [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), elements $s_\ell$ and adelic elements $sf_\ell$ indexed by the primes $\ell\neq r,\bar r$ with the four clauses `hs` (at places not above $r$ the element $sf_\ell$ localises to $s_\ell\otimes 1$; at places above $r$ it localises to $1$; the diagonal image of $\ell$ times $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` if $\ell\mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell)=\ell$), and the groups $\Gamma t_\ell=\Gamma t\cap s_\ell\Gamma t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). A unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma t$ (`hwbar`). Moreover `hΛℤ`: all rational integers lie in $\Lambda$, and a coordinate map $coord:\Lambda\to\mathbb Z_{r^2}\times\mathbb Z_{r^2}$ with `hcoord : IsOrderCoord` (additive, unital, twisted-multiplicative, injective, dense and trace-compatible).
--
--   The special fibre. A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $Onr/\pi$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of dimension $2$ along the unit section of $A_0$, with `hA₀ : A₀.IsFormalModuleVia coord X₀ θ₀`; families $e,e'$ of endomorphisms of $A_0$ indexed by $\Gamma t$, over the base (`he`), with degrees $\deg$. The hypotheses `hE1`, `hE1mul`, `hE1sc`, `hE1'`, `hE2`, `hE3`, `hE4` (summarised here) state: each $e_\gamma$ is one half of an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level-$N$ structure; multiplicativity of $\gamma\mapsto e_\gamma$ up to powers of $r$; scalars $\gamma$ give multiplication maps up to powers of $r$, and conversely only scalars do; for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $X_0$-action and $\varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ such that each $e_\gamma$ is induced on formal coordinates by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser with $E_0\varepsilon=r^{k_\gamma}\,g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$; the action of $\Gamma t$ on a full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ is given by labels in $\Lambda$, multiplicative and unital modulo $n$; and every level-preserving $r$-power isogeny endomorphism of a curve pulled back from $A_0$ over an algebraically closed field comes from some $e_\gamma$ up to powers of $r$.
--
--   Quaternionic endomorphism data on $A_0$. An order $R_2\le R_1$ (`hR₂`, `hR₂o`) with $r$-power denominators over $R_1$ (`hR₂r`) and endomorphisms $\hat e$ of $A_0$ over the base (`hê`); the clauses `hE5a`, `hE5b1`, `hE5b2`, `hE5b3`, `hE5c`, `hE5d`, `hE5e`, `hE5g` (summarised here) make $x\mapsto\hat e_x$ additive on points, $\Lambda$-equivariant and level preserving, unital, multiplicative, compatible with integers and with conjugation and reduced norms, compatible with $e_\gamma$ up to powers of $r$, simultaneously matched with $\iota_0$ through one and the same $g$ as in `hE2`, and stable under the main involution. A second maximal order $\Lambda_{1s}$ with $R_1\le\Lambda_{1s}$ and $\Lambda_1\cap\Lambda_{1s}=R_1$ (`hΛ₁s`, `hR₁Λ₁s`, `htwin`), an order $R_2'\le\Lambda_{1s}$ with $r$-power denominators (`hR₂'`, `hR₂'o`, `hR₂'r`) containing $R_2$ (`hR₂R₂'`), and endomorphisms $\hat e'$ over the base (`hê'`) satisfying the analogous clauses `hE6a`, `hE6b1`, `hE6b2`, `hE6b3`, `hE6c`, `hE6d` (extending $\hat e$), `hE6e1`, `hE6e2` (injectivity) and `hE6f` (an element killing the $q$-torsion is divisible by $q$ up to $r$-powers, for primes $q\neq r$).
--
--   The formal moduli package. A ring homomorphism $\iota:\mathbb Z_{r^2}\to Onr$; a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ of height $4$ (`hΦ`, `hΦ4`); a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`); a family $\eta$ sending rigidified objects to $MD$-points, with the three clauses `hη`: on admissible rigidifications over Noetherian bases $\eta$ identifies equality with isomorphy, $\eta$ is compatible with base change, and every $MD$-point is locally of the form $\eta(t)$ for admissible $t$ on a Zariski cover. An injective homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $\Phi$-action and $\varpi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image has bounded denominators (`hE₀`). Further, a family $eD$ with `hnatD` (naturality), `hbijD` (bijectivity), `hfstD` (it preserves the structure morphism $\psi$), `hGLD` (the abstract $\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$-action `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy` corresponds to the twisted action `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct`), `hPiD` ($\pi$-translates correspond to Frobenius twisting of the first coordinate) and `hPiexD` (existence of $\pi$-translates): thus $eD$ identifies the functor of $MD$-points with $(Onr\to\cdot)\times\Omega$, where $\Omega$ is the functor of Deligne data over $\mathcal O$, $K_0$, $\pi$. A homomorphism $\kappa$ from $Onr/\pi$ to $Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), a natural number $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to $X_0$ transported by $\kappa$ (`hβ₀`). A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   The $\bar r$-Atkin–Lehner datum on $A_0$: a curve $A_{0w}$, morphisms $aw$, $aw'$, $bw$, $bw'$ over the base with the stated triangles, a natural number $k_w$, `hALw` (the pair $(aw,aw')$ is an Atkin–Lehner quotient at $\bar r$), `hBSw` (isogeny pair of degree $r^{k_w}$), `hBSwlev` (level preservation) and `hJOINTw` (for every injective $E_0$ as above there is a single $g$ matching all $e_\gamma$ with $\iota_0(\gamma)$ and the composite $aw\circ bw$, in diagrammatic order $aw$ followed by $bw$, with $\iota_0(\bar w)$, all up to powers of $r$).
--
--   The comparison map. A family $\Xi$ sending rigidified curves over Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras to $MD$-points, subject to `hΞleg` (over a connected base the structure morphism of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (naturality under base change of rigidified curves), `hΞiso` (invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves matching the rigidifications up to powers of $r$) and `hΞdef` (explicit description of $\Xi$ through $\eta$, an index $j\le 1$, an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.X=X$ and a rigidity transport relation `IsRigTransport` with respect to $\theta_0$, $\kappa$, $\beta_0$). An element $g_0\in\mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and the three matching hypotheses `heqΓ`, `heqW`, `heqS`: translating a rigidification by $e_\gamma$, passing to an $\bar r$-Atkin–Lehner quotient, and passing to an $\ell$-level isogeny quotient correspond, under $\Xi$, to the action of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$, of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\bar w)g_0^{-1}$, and of $r^{c}\,g_0\iota_0(\ell s^{-1})g_0^{-1}$ respectively, for some integer $c$; the third is stated for arbitrary data $n_1$, $\ell$, $s$, $sf$, a level-$\ell$ subgroup $C_0$ of $A_0$ and the accompanying isogeny and joint-matching hypotheses.
--
--   The family at full level $n$. A homomorphism $\theta t:\Gamma t\to G$ and a family $\Theta f$ assigning to each $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ and each triple $((\psi,P),g)$ with $\psi:Onr\to B$, $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$ and $g\in G$, a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, subject to: `hnat` (naturality), `hG` (right $G$-equivariance through $\rho$), `hi'` (invariance under the twisted action of $\gamma\in\Gamma t$ combined with multiplication by $\theta t(\gamma)$), `het` (unique infinitesimal lifting along surjections with multiplicatively trivial kernel), `hfib` (the fibre criterion over algebraically closed fields: two triples have the same image exactly when they differ by some $\gamma\in\Gamma t$, with the corresponding pullback of Deligne data and the prescribed Frobenius twist of $\psi$), `hC0e`, `hC0u` (existence and uniqueness of the normalised level-$n$ transport of $P_0$), `hC1e`, `hC1u`, `hC1p` (existence, uniqueness up to isomorphism, and base change of a curve with rigidification whose $\Xi$-image matches a given $(\psi,P)$ under the $g_0^{-1}$-twisted action), `hC2` and `hC2c` (the value of $\Theta f$ at $g=1$ on such data is the $ptF$-point of the curve with its transported level-$n$ structure, and composing with $p$ gives the $pt$-point of the curve), `hC3`, `hC4`, `hC4pi` (the effect of the $\Gamma t$-action, of a general element of the quaternion units through $g_0$, and of a $\pi$-translate, each producing a new rigidification matching the translated Deligne datum, with the level-$n$ structures twisted by $\chi(\theta t(\gamma))$ and the extra level-$\ell$ structures determined), and `hC5` (over algebraically closed fields an isomorphism of the two curves is induced by some $\gamma\in\Gamma t$, compatibly with level-$n$ structures and with level-$\ell$ subgroups).
--
--   The tower data on the special fibre. For each prime $\ell\neq r,\bar r$: a level-$\ell$ subgroup $C_0(\ell)$ of $A_0$, a quotient curve $A_{0s}(\ell)$ with morphisms $as$, $as'$, $bs$, $bs'$ over the base, natural numbers $ks(\ell)$, and the hypotheses `hLIs` ($\ell$-level isogeny via $as,as'$), `hBSs` (isogeny pair of degree $r^{ks(\ell)}$), `hBSslev` (level preservation) and `hJOINTs` (joint matching of all $e_\gamma$ with $\iota_0(\gamma)$ and of $as$ followed by $bs$ with $\iota_0(\mathrm{star}(s_\ell))$ through one $g$, up to powers of $r$); `hSTAB`: an element $\gamma\in\Gamma t$ stabilises $C_0(\ell)$ under $e_\gamma$ if and only if $\gamma\in\Gamma t_\ell$. The hypotheses `hCLe` and `hCLu` give existence and uniqueness (as subfunctors of points) of the extra level-$\ell$ structure on a curve $E$ which corresponds, through a rigidification, to a prescribed level-$\ell$ structure on $A_0$. Families $\Theta T_\ell$ of $B$-points of $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ on $(\psi,P)$, natural (`hnatT`), whose values are computed by `hVT`: whenever $(E,\varrho)$ matches $(\psi,P)$ through $\Xi$ and the $g_0^{-1}$-twisted action, and $C$ corresponds to $C_0(\ell)$ through $\varrho$, the morphism underlying $\Theta T_\ell(B)(\psi,P)$ is the $ptT_\ell$-point of $(E,C)$.
--
--   The level-$\ell$ fine moduli. A fixed prime $\ell\neq r,\bar r$; a scheme $M_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point map $ptF_\ell$ on pairs (full level-$n$ structure, extra level-$\ell$ structure) and `hMℓ : IsFineModuliT`; a morphism $\pi_\ell:M_\ell\to M$ over the base (`hπℓf`) forgetting the level-$\ell$ structure on points (`hπℓ`); a morphism $p_\ell:M_\ell\to\mathcal Y_\ell$ over the base (`hpℓg`) inducing on points the $ptT_\ell$-point of the curve with its level-$\ell$ subgroup (`hpℓ`); an action $\rho_\ell$ of $G$ on $M_\ell$ over the base (`hρℓf`), compatible with $\pi_\ell$ (`hρℓπ`), leaving $p_\ell$ invariant (`hρℓp`) and realising level-$n$ twisting with unchanged level-$\ell$ subgroup on points (`hρℓtw`). Finally a family $\Theta f_\ell$ assigning to each Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ and each triple $((\psi,P),g)$ a $B$-point of $M_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, which is natural (`hnatℓ`) and whose value at $g=1$ is computed by `hvalℓ`: whenever $(E,\varrho)$ matches $(\psi,P)$ through $\Xi$ and the $g_0^{-1}$-twisted action, $P_n$ is the normalised level-$n$ transport of $P_0$ along $\varrho$, and $C$ corresponds to $C_0(\ell)$ through $\varrho$, the morphism underlying $\Theta f_\ell(B)((\psi,P),1)$ is the $ptF_\ell$-point of $(E,P_n)$ together with $C$.
--
--   Conclusion. For every commutative Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, and every pair $x=(\psi,P)$ consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\psi:Onr\to B$ and a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$, the $B$-point $\Theta f_\ell(B)(x,1)$ of $M_\ell$, pushed forward along $p_\ell$ by the natural transformation `Scheme.nilpPoints.mapHom fMℓ (g ℓ) pℓ hpℓg` — that is, the morphism $\operatorname{Spec}B\to M_\ell$ underlying $\Theta f_\ell(B)(x,1)$ followed by $p_\ell$ — equals $\Theta T_\ell(B)(x)$ as a $B$-point of $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$.
--
--   This is the compatibility step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison which says that the formal family of points at full level $n$ and level $\ell$ lies over the formal family on the Hecke-tower curve $\mathcal Y_\ell$: composing with the forgetful morphism $p_\ell$ recovers $\Theta T_\ell$. It is used in the construction of a level-$\ell$ fine family with prescribed value at the identity of $G$, which in turn feeds the identification of the tower of Shimura curves with the quotient of Drinfeld's $r$-adic upper half plane.
